A||||6||fiero56||Driving directions||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||11-20-2000||11:00 AM||||I know how to use mapquest and similar, but here is the question.<P>I am heading out for Oregon(location is about 45 miles North of California)in one week. Several people have told me to try to stay to the south because of moutain passes and snow, etc. I will probably be heading across most of the country on I-70 or I-80, but need info on what to do about the mountains etc. Will either be taking dad's Sebring outitted for towing a trailer, or mom's mini-van which is already setup for trailer towing. The minivan has trac control(for what its worth), the Sebring doesn't. Any thoughts about what routes to take or avoid.||||reg||5
Z||000001||william_thorniii||11-20-2000||05:13 PM||||56,<P>I have a buddy that used to drive from Spokane, WA to Flint, MI and another that used to drive back and forth from Flint to Colorado Springs every three months.  They took different routes.  My buddy in CO took I-70 all the way out.  Says it is an AWFUL drive (23 hours straight from Flint... 4.5 hours N. of Dayton) because it is so flat, boring, etc.  The guy heading to WA would always head through Montana.  Then again, he grew up in Montana, so is a bit partial.  The scenery is much better.  I would think this time of year the difference between a couple of hundred miles between the Northern and Southern route would be a wash since it is cold as hell that high anyway.  <P>I will gladly contact both of them if you would like for me to.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-21-2000||09:02 PM||||Drew--Mountain passes this time of year can be a real bit ch, but don't count on staying out of the weather just because you might be a little farther south (I 70).  I had a regular run (used to drive a truck) from the Motor City to KC (4x/week) for about a year.  Every time I thought I'd stay out of the weather by taking 70, I'd find myself on ice whereas some of the guys running on 80 stayed on dry roads.  Don't worry about the snow.  Be very careful of the ice, though.  If it gets too bad, pull over.  I hope you have a safe trip.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||11-22-2000||02:37 AM||||Drive carefully Drew, whichever route you take -- I do know that at this time of year in the higher elevations they close parts of I-80 or at least chains are required (around Tahoe) - they have already had snow above the 2,500 foot level but it hasn't presented any problems thus far. You may also run into an area around Denver if you go that way - to the north and west of Denver it can get bad at times. <P>Hey, if it gets too bad just pull over and ski!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||11-22-2000||09:25 AM||||Instead of Mapquest, you can try Tellme or 1-800-MY-YAHOO...<P>Both have driving directions available.<P>I've used Tellme (1-800-555-TELL) many times...just tell it over the phone your starting point and your destination (including street address) and it will read driving directions to you over the phone.<P>Works great and it is updated to include road closings and weather advisories.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87ECO5||11-22-2000||10:03 AM||||Is it official, Drew, or is this just a visit?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||11-22-2000||10:22 AM||||It's just a visit for me, but it's "official" for dad. I'm just heading out to help with the driving, unpacking etc. He already found a place to stay, but has decided he doesn't want to try to drive a convertible 'cross country with a trailer. Sooooo, he has decided that he wants to get either a truck, or, gasp(!!!)an SUV, something he has been strongly opposed to ever since they became popular. All this, and we leave Tuesday.||||reg||1
